.TH PERROR 3
.SH NAME
perror, strerror, errno, sys_errlist, sys_nerr \- system error messages
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
perror(s)
char *s;

char *
strerror(errnum)
int errnum;

extern int errno;
extern char *sys_errlist[];
extern int sys_nerr;
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
When a system call fails, it returns \-1 and sets the external variable
.B errno
to a number that tells why. <errno.h> gives the numbers names, such as
ENOENT, "No such file or directory", or EACCES, "Permission denied".
errno is not cleared when a call succeeds, so it means something only
just after a call has failed.
.PP
.B perror
writes a message on the standard error: the string
.IR s ,
a colon and a space, the message for the present value of errno, and a
newline. If
.I s
is NULL or empty, only the message and the newline are written. The
argument is usually the name of the program, or of the file the call
was about.
.PP
.B strerror
returns the message for the error number
.IR errnum .
.PP
The messages are in the array
.BR sys_errlist ,
indexed by the error number;
.B sys_nerr
is the number of messages in it. A number outside it has the message
"Unknown error".
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
	if ((fd = open(name, 0)) < 0) {
		perror(name);
		exit(1);
	}
.fi
.PP
prints, for instance,
.PP
.nf
	notes: No such file or directory
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
intro(2)
.SH HISTORY
The
.B perror
function first appeared in Version 4 AT&T UNIX.
.PP
.B strerror
is not in the C libraries of Version 7 AT&T UNIX, System V or 4.3BSD.
